{
 "id": "dense_connective",
 "title": "Dense connective tissue (tendon)",
 "node": "dense_connective_scene",
 "tag": "Inside a tendon",
 "cell": "animal",
 "blurb": "A slice of one tendon bundle (fascicle): wavy collagen fibres lie side by side, with rows of flat fibroblasts, a few elastic fibres and little blood supply. Click a part.",
 "note": "Schematic, not a micrograph. Drawn is a tendon-type arrangement: fibres parallel to each other and to the pull. In the sclera of the eye and in the dermis of the skin the same collagen is interwoven in bundles that run in many directions, so those tissues look different from this one. Real sizes: collagen fibrils 50-500 nm across (the 200 nm fibrils are drawn at that true width, so they look like hairlines until you zoom in), collagen fibres 10-50 um across (22 um drawn), fascicles 100-500 um across (about 290 um drawn), crimp wavelength about 100 um and fibroblast length about 27 um (both measured in mouse-tail tendon at 6 weeks, a small tendon of another species), and a capillary lumen 5-10 um across (drawn 10 um outer width, wall not drawn separately). Illustrative: the 10 degree lean of the crimp, the fibroblast width and thickness (8 um), the number and placement of cells (fewer than in mouse tail; cells sit in the gaps between fibre columns, as real tenocytes sit between bundles), the elastic fibre width (1.5 um) and count (three), the position of the capillary and of fibrils (37 shown of many thousands per fibre), the length of fibrils drawn, the depth of the slab and the colours. Ground substance is drawn as a pale slab and has no material; the fibres are drawn as plain rods although each is a bundle of fibrils, and as short straight pieces joined end to end, whereas real fibres are far longer. Elastic fibres are drawn straight; in real tendon they follow the crimp. The fibrils at the top are a separate inset showing a few of the fibrils inside one fibre, not part of the slab. The slab has no sheath of loose connective tissue (endotenon) around it, which real fascicles have, and in very small tendons such as mouse tail a fascicle may not exist as a distinct level. The capillary is drawn inside the slab for clarity; sources do not say where vessels lie, and in real tendon they run in the connective tissue sheaths. This tendon-type scene does not stand for the sclera, which is interwoven in lamellae.",
 "bg": "#f3efe6",
